notes.body:
Value semantics means `a = b` and `return x` make **copies**. For a type that owns heap memory (a string, a vector of orders, a 1 GB price buffer) a copy means `new` + `memcpy` + later `delete`: microseconds, cache pollution, and allocator contention. Before C++11 the language had no way to say "this object is about to die, just steal its buffer", so:

- Returning a big `std::vector` by value was a **deep copy** unless the compiler happened to apply RVO.
- `v.push_back(std::string(...))` copied the temporary and then destroyed it.
- Growing a `vector<std::string>` copied every string into the new buffer.
- People contorted APIs into out-parameters (`void build(std::vector<Order>& out)`), and `std::auto_ptr` "moved" on copy, which broke containers.

C++11 added **rvalue references** (`T&&`) and **move semantics**: overload on "this argument is a temporary" and transfer ownership with a few pointer assignments.

**In class (Lec-8, custom String):** we built a heap-backed `String` (`char* mPtr; uint mSize;`) and a `concat()` that returns `String("…")` by value, then disassembled it with `objdump -d -M intel`. The listing shows the real cost model:

```cpp
// concat():  call String::String(char const*)     ; build the temporary at [rbp-0x30]
//            call String::String(String&&)         ; move it into the hidden return slot (rdi)
//            call String::~String()                ; destroy the temporary
// main():    call concat(...)                      ; result lands in another temporary
//            call String::String(String&&)         ; move again into c
```

The return slot is passed as a hidden pointer in `rdi`: the *caller* reserves the stack space for the result. Two move constructor calls appear because that build had copy elision turned off (as with `-std=c++14 -fno-elide-constructors` at `-O0`). Compiled as plain C++17, `return String("…")` and `String c{concat(a, b)}` are **guaranteed elisions**: the program prints only three "const* params constructor" lines and three destructors, with zero moves. "Loving the compiler" means knowing what it will elide for you.

Inside `template<class T> void wrapper(T&& arg)`, `arg` is a **named** variable, so it's always an lvalue. To pass it on with its **original** category, use `std::forward<T>(arg)`: a **conditional** cast to rvalue that fires only if the caller passed an rvalue (`T` is a non-reference type).

**In class (Lec-8 `utils.h` + `checking.cpp`):** we wrote our own `Forward` on top of our `Remove_Ref` trait:

```cpp
template <typename T>
T&& Forward(typename Remove_Ref<T>::type& obj) { return static_cast<T&&>(obj); }

struct X {
    void print() const &  { std::cout << "L value\n"; }   // ref-qualified overloads
    void print() const && { std::cout << "R value\n"; }
};
template <typename T> void func(T&& obj) { utils::Forward<T>(obj).print(); }

const X a;
func(a);      // T = const X&  -> Forward returns const X&  -> "L value"
func(X{});    // T = X         -> Forward returns X&&       -> "R value"
```

Why the parameter is `typename Remove_Ref<T>::type&`: it's a **non-deduced context**, which forces you to spell `Forward<T>` explicitly. If `T` were deduced from the argument, it would always see an lvalue. (The real `std::forward` also has an rvalue overload, plus `constexpr` and `noexcept`.) Without forwarding, `obj.print()` always prints "L value".

Perfect forwarding in the standard library: `emplace_back(Args&&...)`, `std::make_unique<T>(Args&&...)`, `std::thread`'s constructor:

```cpp
template <typename T, typename... Args>
T* construct_in_pool(void* slot, Args&&... args) {
    return ::new (slot) T(std::forward<Args>(args)...);   // each arg keeps its category
}
```

**`std::move` vs `std::forward`:** move = *unconditional* cast to rvalue (use it on things you own and are done with). forward = *conditional* cast (use it only on forwarding references, exactly once per path).

If a class manages a resource, the compiler-generated special members do the wrong (shallow) thing.

- **Rule of 3 (C++98):** if you need any of destructor, copy constructor, copy assignment, you need all three.
- **Rule of 5 (C++11):** add move constructor and move assignment. Declaring a destructor or copy op **suppresses the implicit move operations** (moves silently fall back to copies).
- **Rule of 0:** best of all, hold resources in members that already manage themselves (`std::string`, `std::vector`, `std::unique_ptr`) and declare none of the five.

```cpp
class String {
    std::size_t mSize = 0;          // declared first: mPtr's initialiser uses mSize
    char*       mPtr  = nullptr;
public:
    String() = default;
    String(const char* s) : mSize(std::strlen(s)), mPtr(new char[mSize + 1]) {
        std::memcpy(mPtr, s, mSize + 1);                       // keep the '\0'
    }
    ~String() { delete[] mPtr; }                               // 1. destructor
    String(const String& o) : mSize(o.mSize), mPtr(new char[o.mSize + 1]) {
        std::memcpy(mPtr, o.mPtr, mSize + 1);                  // 2. deep copy
    }
    String(String&& o) noexcept                                // 4. steal
        : mSize(std::exchange(o.mSize, 0)), mPtr(std::exchange(o.mPtr, nullptr)) {}
    String& operator=(String o) noexcept {                     // 3 + 5: copy-and-swap,
        swap(o); return *this;                                 // by-value param = copy OR move
    }
    void swap(String& o) noexcept { std::swap(mPtr, o.mPtr); std::swap(mSize, o.mSize); }
};
```

(Watch the member order: members are initialised in **declaration order**, not initialiser-list order. If `mPtr` were declared first, `new char[mSize + 1]` would read an uninitialised `mSize`; `-Wreorder` warns about mismatched lists.)

Copy-and-swap gives the strong exception guarantee and handles self-assignment for free; the cost is one extra move. A hand-written `operator=(const String&)` must check `this != &o`, allocate **before** freeing (exception safety), then `delete[]` the old buffer.

**In class (Lec-8):** the custom String had a copy constructor and a move constructor but (a) the move ctor wrote `other->mSize` (`other` is a reference, so it must be `other.mSize`: compile error as written), (b) the destructor only nulled the pointer and **never called `delete[]`** (a leak), (c) no copy/move assignment, so `s1 = s2` would shallow-copy the pointer, (d) it used `uint` (a POSIX typedef; use `std::size_t`/`uint32_t`) and stored no `'\0'`.

**Substitution Failure Is Not An Error:** when substituting template arguments into a function template's **signature** fails, that overload is silently removed from the candidate set instead of causing an error. It's used to enable or disable overloads by type properties.

```cpp
// integral prices: exact arithmetic
template <typename T, std::enable_if_t<std::is_integral_v<T>, int> = 0>
T midPrice(T bid, T ask) { return bid + (ask - bid) / 2; }

// floating prices
template <typename T, std::enable_if_t<std::is_floating_point_v<T>, int> = 0>
T midPrice(T bid, T ask) { return (bid + ask) * 0.5; }

// detection idiom: does T have .size()?
template <typename, typename = void> struct HasSize : std::false_type {};
template <typename T>
struct HasSize<T, std::void_t<decltype(std::declval<T&>().size())>> : std::true_type {};
```

`std::enable_if<cond, T>::type` exists only when `cond` is true, so a false condition makes substitution fail. `std::declval<T>()` pretends to produce a `T` in unevaluated contexts (`decltype`, `sizeof`) without needing a constructor.

Only the **immediate context** (the signature, template parameter defaults, the return type) is SFINAE-friendly. An error **inside the function body** is a hard error.

Concepts are named compile-time predicates on template parameters: readable SFINAE with much better error messages.

```cpp
#include <concepts>
template <typename T>
concept PriceType = std::integral<T> || std::floating_point<T>;

template <typename V>
concept Venue = requires(V v, const Order& o) {
    { v.send(o) } -> std::same_as<void>;     // must have send(const Order&)
    { v.name() }  -> std::convertible_to<std::string_view>;
};

template <Venue V>                       // constrained template parameter
void postOrder(V& v, const Order& o) { v.send(o); }

void logPx(PriceType auto px);           // abbreviated function template

template <typename T> requires std::movable<T>
class RingBuffer { /* ... */ };
```

Benefits over SFINAE: an error says "constraint `Venue<Foo>` not satisfied because `v.send(o)` is invalid" instead of 200 lines of instantiation backtrace; overloads constrained by more specific concepts are **preferred automatically** (subsumption); zero runtime cost. Useful standard concepts: `std::integral`, `std::floating_point`, `std::same_as`, `std::convertible_to`, `std::movable`, `std::copyable`, `std::invocable`, `std::ranges::range`.

Type traits are class templates whose `::value` is computed at compile time using **specialisation** and SFINAE. Writing them is a standard interview question.

```cpp
// is_same: primary false, partial specialisation true
template <class A, class B> struct is_same       : std::false_type {};
template <class A>          struct is_same<A, A> : std::true_type {};

// is_destructible (simplified, for object types): is `declval<T&>().~T()` well-formed?
template <class T, class = void>
struct is_destructible : std::false_type {};
template <class T>
struct is_destructible<T, std::void_t<decltype(std::declval<T&>().~T())>> : std::true_type {};

// is_move_constructible: can T be constructed from an rvalue T?
template <class T>
struct is_move_constructible : std::is_constructible<T, T&&> {};
// is_constructible itself is detected with decltype(::new T(std::declval<Args>()...))
```

What they really mean:
- `is_move_constructible<T>` is **true for a copy-only type** too: `T(const T&)` happily binds an rvalue. It's false only if the move ctor is **deleted** (or the copy ctor is unusable as well).
- `is_nothrow_move_constructible<T>` is what `std::vector` checks before choosing to move on reallocation.
- `is_destructible<T>` is false for `void`, functions, arrays of unknown bound and types with a deleted/private destructor; true for references.
- `is_trivially_copyable<T>` tells you `memcpy` is safe: this is how you write a fast serializer path.

Use them in `static_assert` to lock in performance guarantees:

```cpp
static_assert(std::is_nothrow_move_constructible_v<Order>, "Order must move cheaply");
static_assert(std::is_trivially_copyable_v<MdMsg>, "MdMsg is sent with memcpy");
```

- **Return by value**: RVO/NRVO/implicit move make it the cheapest and clearest option. Out-params are a pre-C++11 habit.
- **Mark moves `noexcept`** (and `= default` them where possible) so containers move instead of copy.
- **Sink parameters:** for a constructor that stores its argument, take it **by value and move** it: one copy for lvalues, zero for rvalues.

```cpp
struct Instrument {
    std::string sym;
    explicit Instrument(std::string s) : sym(std::move(s)) {}
};
```

- Read-only parameters: `const T&` (or `std::string_view` / `std::span`).
- `reserve()` before a known number of `push_back`s; `emplace_back(args...)` constructs in place (no temporary).
- Don't `std::move` a `const` object, and don't `std::move` the return of a local.
- Let the compiler tell you: `-Wall -Wextra -Wpessimizing-move -Wredundant-move`, sanitizers (`-fsanitize=address,undefined`), and Compiler Explorer to read the generated assembly.
- Use `static_assert` with type traits to make performance assumptions compile-time checks.
- `[[nodiscard]]` on functions whose result must not be ignored (e.g. `bool tryPush`).

