short.modelAnswer:
```cpp
Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept
    : mData(other.mData), mSize(other.mSize) {   // steal the pointer: O(1), no allocation
    other.mData = nullptr;                        // leave the source empty but valid,
    other.mSize = 0;                              // so its destructor's delete[] is a no-op
}

Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept {
    if (this != &other) {                         // self-assignment check (x = std::move(x))
        delete[] mData;                           // release what we currently own
        mData = other.mData;                      // steal
        mSize = other.mSize;
        other.mData = nullptr;                    // reset the source
        other.mSize = 0;
    }
    return *this;
}
```

Both are `noexcept` because they only copy pointers. That matters for performance: `std::vector<Buffer>` will move (not copy) elements on reallocation only if the move constructor is noexcept. Nulling the source prevents a double `delete[]` when both destructors run.

