short.modelAnswer:
```cpp
template <class A, class B> struct is_same       : std::false_type {};
template <class A>          struct is_same<A, A> : std::true_type  {};   // partial specialisation

template <class T, class = void>
struct is_destructible : std::false_type {};
template <class T>
struct is_destructible<T, std::void_t<decltype(std::declval<T&>().~T())>> : std::true_type {};
```

Technique: the primary template gives the default answer (`false`). A partial specialisation matches only when its pattern fits: `<A, A>` when both arguments are the same type. For `is_destructible`, `std::declval<T&>()` pretends to have a `T` in an unevaluated `decltype`, and `.~T()` checks whether the destructor is callable. If it is, `void_t<...>` is `void` and the specialisation matches the default `void` argument; if not, **SFINAE** (substitution failure) silently discards the specialisation and the primary `false` is used. `is_move_constructible<T>` is built the same way, as `is_constructible<T, T&&>`.

